From 1f3e84bbeaaa39bb6fddfcca71e64a00e5b5548a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: cpkt9762 <cpkt9762@163.com>
Date: Mon, 24 Aug 2026 06:18:33 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] feat(cloud): serve over TLS and allow dropping the dashboard

cloud serve only ever listened in the clear, which is fine behind a
tunnel and not fine on a published port. Payload sealing does not close
that gap: the bearer token rides in a request header and the project
name in the query string, so neither is covered, and an attacker who
captures the token can pull everything without decrypting anything.

Server: WithTLS switches Start to ListenAndServeTLS. A half-configured
pair is ignored rather than honoured, so a missing key cannot leave a
listener that looks encrypted but is not.

Client: ENGRAM_CLOUD_CA_FILE points at the certificate to trust. Go
offers no environment knob for this on macOS -- it defers to the
platform verifier, so SSL_CERT_FILE is ignored -- and installing the CA
as a system trust root would make every program on the machine trust it.
The pool replaces the system roots rather than extending them, and a
missing or unparseable file is an error: falling back would silently
drop the pinning. InsecureSkipVerify is never used.

WithoutDashboard drops the browser routes. Everything under /sync and
/admin sits behind withAuth, but dashboard.Mount and the bootstrap pages
answer before authentication, and on an internet-facing listener that is
the entire pre-auth surface. A deployment that never opens the dashboard
has no reason to carry it. ENGRAM_CLOUD_DASHBOARD=0 selects this;
default stays on so upstream behaviour is unchanged.

The first attempt at the toggle only guarded the HandleFunc block and
left dashboard.Mount registering /dashboard/ regardless, which the tests
caught. Mutation testing confirms all four behaviours are load-bearing.
